"""(v2_2_0, task TK_Deck13_Ex12_Build: ONLY example 12 differs from examples_v2_0_0.py - the two refusals are cut with removeprefix so that they read without the node's prefix Error: ; the file examples_v2_0_0.py stays unchanged.)
Every code listing of the SEN0401 chapter 13 deck 2.0.0 (Bitcoin Security; a NEW file, the deck 1.2.0 and its examples_v1_2_0.py stay unchanged) and the source of the chapter-code
manifest chapter_code_ch13_v1_0_0.json that the page's chapter-code tab reads. One entry per code slide, in SLIDE ORDER. A listing is a list of GROUPS; a group is a few single-line
statements and the plain-language note that explains them (the deck standard asks for an explanation beside every code box). Every statement is ONE line. The last row of a listing is an
expression (`final`, which is also in `show`): the slide prints its value under it, the only REPL text of the slide; the full result of the example is drawn as a chart or a table beside
the listing from the recorded `show` values (examples_run_v2_0_0.py), never typed. A statement is at most 56 characters, so that a code line never wraps (deck standard D12).
Every example uses only the chapter library of the page (the wheel sen0401_ch13_lib-1.1.0: the module sen0401_ch13_lib_v1_0_0 and the saved files it reads, which the wheel carries).
The values read from R are the record of ONE run of Bitcoin Core 31.1 on regtest (so they are Recorded on the slides); `seen` finds a passage in a saved source; every other value is computed
by the statements. No example prints an error first: an example that teaches a refusal reads the recorded refusal as text and prints a plain line first."""
__version__ = "2.2.0"

